WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.210
To drive one of London's
famous black cabs,

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.493
you need to be a special kind of person.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.520
London's cabbies don't use GPS,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.600
but then, they don't need it.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.113
They have the Knowledge.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.500
I have undertaken the
stiffest geographical test

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.260
that any transport
operative has to go through,

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.810
probably anywhere in the world.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.960
It consists of memorizing

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.550
straight line routes across London.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.740
You learn something like 25,000 streets,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.580
left this street, right this street,

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.630
forward this roundabout,
leave by this exit,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.263
probably a hundred thousand
points of interest.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.050
On average, it takes four and a half years

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.170
to complete the Knowledge

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.593
and only one in seven students passes.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.760
Those expert navigators that
do are remarkable specimens.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.003
They're even studied by scientists.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.870
And while all our brains are excellent

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.610
at decoding our surroundings,

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.650
good design makes navigating
a whole lot easier

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.746
in complex places like cities.

00:01:06.746 --> 00:01:10.227
This is all part of a
process called wayfinding.

00:01:10.227 --> 00:01:13.480
Wayfinding is about finding your way

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.770
while you're interacting
with your surroundings.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.350
The environment can help
us or can hinder us.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.060
The information that you've got

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.860
can be useful or it can be absent.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.470
From the roads we drive,
the paths we walk,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.360
to negotiating busy
airports and shopping malls,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.110
carefully designed wayfinding
systems are everywhere,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.230
but in a world where GPS
is in all of our pockets,

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:39.776
do we risk losing our innate
ability to find our way?

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.430
Wayfinding's kind of the
opposite of being lost,

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.030
so when you know where you're going,

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.670
or you work out how you go somewhere,

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:00.260
then you are actually using
the process of wayfinding.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.440
It is innate in humans.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.070
As creatures, we come from the savannah

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.980
and we always needed to know

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:11.530
where our food was, where
water was, where the camp was,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.530
so our ability to navigate
is fundamental to survival.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.180
Humans are spacial beings.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.380
Our brains give us an internal compass

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.620
to help us understand and
navigate unfamiliar environments.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.400
Neuroscientists talk
about the cognitive map,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.630
which is the map that
we have in our brain.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:38.350
It effectively works in the
same way as a regular map

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.650
because it allows us to retain a memory,

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.675
and it allows us, for
example, to take shortcuts.

00:02:44.675 --> 00:02:47.425
Research has found very
specific types of cells

00:02:47.425 --> 00:02:48.890
in our hippocampus

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.840
that fire in different locations
within our environment.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:57.520
Place cells cover our
environment with a firing pattern

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.730
that we believe helps support
the idea of a cognitive map,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.880
as in we have this,

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.940
not just a physical
representation of our environment

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.350
that we can kind of see,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.540
but actually a neural representation

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.520
of cells that fire in a
pattern in our environment

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.010
that helps us reduce the
cognitive demand needed

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.700
to actually travel from
point A to point B.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.830
Likewise, grid cells are
also found in this region,

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:28.450
and they form a
hexadirectional representation

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.950
of a familiar, learned environment.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.580
So for example, as I'm
sitting in this room,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:39.580
I would have a grid-like
pattern of cells that fire,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.810
that then move as I move around the room.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.410
You kind of think of it
as, like, pointillism.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.390
The combination of all
of these different cells

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.360
that fire for different places

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.940
and for different directions
that you're approaching,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.650
barriers and such in your environment,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.503
build up the idea that we
have this cognitive map.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.440
Despite the brilliance of our brains,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.330
it's still very easy to get lost

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.610
in our busy, ever-changing cities.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.190
Cities are very stressful places.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.940
There's a lot of noise, tall
buildings, people around.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.070
London is notorious for
being a difficult place

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.670
to find your way through,

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:26.861
and people do get lost in it all the time.

00:04:26.861 --> 00:04:29.280
I couldn't find any of my landmarks.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.160
It provokes horror.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.820
I was lost. You know, I really was lost.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.800
You want to end up in a place

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.690
and you end up in another one,

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.773
so I was late for work because of that.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.960
London is a messy city

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.510
compared to something like New York.

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:50.140
One reason is the sheer scale.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.440
It's a very big city.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.410
It was not designed as
a city at the beginning,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.780
a series of different settlements.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.220
Manhattan is a lot easier,
as a city, to navigate

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.053
than London

00:05:02.053 --> 00:05:05.360
because of that consistent,
uniform grid system.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.530
When people get lost, they
basically run out of clues

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.273
to help them orientate themselves.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.160
Point north? OK.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:17.993
Uh...

00:05:19.595 --> 00:05:20.445
Is it that way?

00:05:21.406 --> 00:05:22.550
Uh...

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.383
The Thames is that-

00:05:24.408 --> 00:05:25.249
No!

00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:26.950
It's the other way.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.564
Yeah? Oh.

00:05:28.564 --> 00:05:31.795
Uh, I'm gonna go that way.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.913
Close enough!

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.370
Navigating in London is so difficult

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:42.140
that 44% of those walking above ground

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:46.180
actually use a map of the
Underground, London's Tube.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.630
It's very accessible.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.710
The Tube map is an excellent device

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.900
for understanding London's whole structure

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.600
and how to connect and
use it across the city.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.080
It's a shorthand.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.060
It gives you a picture of the city.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.000
It gives you a confidence,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.077
but it's not a great
device for walking around.

00:06:03.077 --> 00:06:08.077
The difficulty is when
people use the tube map

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:11.030
to navigate when they get above ground,

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:15.915
because it's not a reflection
of how the city is laid out.

00:06:15.915 --> 00:06:18.840
We found that there's a
lot of people getting lost.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.760
There was a lot of people
who didn't travel places

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.670
because they didn't think they could.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:26.560
When you look down a street

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.890
and it doesn't look very interesting,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.200
or whatever is at the end
of it isn't very visible,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.870
you will naturally think, "Hmm,
maybe I won't go down there.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.090
Let's not adventure."

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.990
Well, the next street could be full

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.900
of wonderful, interesting bohemian stores

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.410
that you want to go and see,

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:45.070
but because you can't see
it, you don't know it exists

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.250
and you don't go down there

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.740
and the city, therefore,
is a quite dark place

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.450
until you get to know it,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.060
and then you know those little shortcuts,

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.750
you know something is round the corner.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.800
But that takes time for
you to build a mental map

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.303
and to understand it,
and have that confidence.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.280
Applied Information
Group is a design agency

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.330
who specialize in fixing
this very problem.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:07.340
They created a tool

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.010
to help people get around the city center:

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:11.620
Legible London.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.420
Legible London is London's walking system.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.490
It started, really, from the observation

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.680
that walking around London,

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.100
you don't get a lot of
help in the last mile.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:27.758
The sign system is designed around

00:07:27.758 --> 00:07:29.437
answering four questions,

00:07:29.437 --> 00:07:31.230
no more than that.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.570
The first question is, "Where am I?"

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.250
So you'll see information at the top

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.010
giving you the village that you're in

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:38.790
and the area within that village

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.700
and there's a map with
a large "You are here"

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.910
to kind of, "Ok, so this is gonna help me,

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:45.430
tell me where I am."

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.610
Second question that it answers
is, "Well, where is it?"

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.450
So we don't know what you're looking for

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:54.880
and this is the reason why it
evolved as a mapping system

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.580
rather than with lots of finger posts,

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:58.290
because on a mapping system,

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:03.290
we can get between 200 and
250 locations on that map.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.430
The third question is,
"Well, how do I get there?"

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.930
And that's why you see
this very careful selection

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.350
of landmarks which are highlighted,

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:15.360
that are designed to
be placed along routes

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.637
so that you can see that,
"I need to go along here,"

00:08:17.637 --> 00:08:20.320
and that's why we started
to introduce 3D buildings,

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:21.920
because we found just naming them

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:23.570
and saying there was a church here,

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:24.990
wasn't doing enough.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.217
And then the fourth question,

00:08:26.217 --> 00:08:27.850
"Well, what else is around here?"

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:30.180
So that's why you have this wider area map

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.020
that goes out to five minutes,
with a lot more detail,

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:35.340
because it actually tells you

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.770
that there are interesting
streets round the corner,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:38.825
that, "Oh, I didn't know

00:08:38.825 --> 00:08:40.725
there was another tube station there."

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:46.630
As you approach this
sign with a map on it,

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.077
it's quite obvious that I'm here,

00:08:48.077 --> 00:08:49.540
the way in which I'm facing

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:51.800
and if you look ahead,
South Molton Street,

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.880
that's South Molton Street.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.260
So immediately, I haven't had to think

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.883
and I'm orienting myself.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.100
The map at the top here
gives me more of an idea

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.860
of what's within 15 minutes.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.280
So if you can see the
number of tube stations,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.330
there's about nine of there
within a 15-minute walk,

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.800
and not a lot of people realize

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.250
that these tube stations
are as close as that

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.860
and it gives you the basic
directions in order to get there

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.010
using these pathways, which
were decided very carefully

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.310
and then, as you're traveling,
we'll be guiding you

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.590
by finding other ones of
these signs along the way.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.190
We also, from research, know that

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:30.490
remembering street names
is very hard to do,

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.390
the reason being is that
street names are quite abstract

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.380
and they don't create
an image in your mind,

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.920
and your memory works so much better

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.293
when you create an image.

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:43.500
You know, one of those secrets
of learning people's names

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.860
is soon as you meet somebody,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.270
you meet a Kevin, just
think of Kevin Bacon,

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:48.310
and then when you meet them next,

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:49.810
you think "Why am I
thinking of Kevin Bacon?

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:50.930
Oh, your name's Kevin."

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:52.240
That's how the brain works,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.135
so that's why street names are poor.

00:09:55.135 --> 00:09:57.940
We just ended up redacting,
taking things out,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:00.090
how can we make this
as simple as possible,

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.660
Because there's so much
information on that map,

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:02.960
it's so detailed,

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:05.633
it can become overwhelming
very, very quickly.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.900
It takes a lot of iteration
to get that balance right,

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.810
to get all of these things in play.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.630
I think we went through about
293 different iterations

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:17.687
to resolve the whole design.

00:10:17.687 --> 00:10:19.830
And the colors on the map were probably

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.440
one of the hardest things
to try and resolve.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.550
Big learning there is

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.090
to try and reduce the number
of colors that you're using.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.860
We came up with the use of blue.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:34.860
It's a color that works well
in U.K.'s overcast skies.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:37.120
We wanted them to be, like,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.640
you can spot them from a distance

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.470
and that's where the idea of a yellow bar

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:42.603
at the top came from.

00:10:42.603 --> 00:10:44.850
It's higher up, it was
yellow in the environment,

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.960
it kind of speaks to
you about information,

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:49.180
and can be very visible

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:51.410
and all the research that we did showed,

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.240
not by a huge factor,

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.640
but the power of the Transport
for London's typeface,

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:57.600
Johnston,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.430
the kind of respect that it had

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:01.400
for giving me transport information,

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:02.960
being part of the transport system,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:04.440
and using Johnston underground,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.440
makes more logical sense.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.110
In the Spiers Lab at
University College London,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:13.260
neuroscientists Sarah and Chris

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.003
are running an experiment
on cab driver, James.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.130
So the next thing we're gonna run

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.780
is a very exciting scan for us.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.060
It's the high resolution
picture of your hippocampus.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:26.722
Would you like to begin?

00:11:26.722 --> 00:11:27.960
Anytime you're ready.

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:29.010
All right, perfect.

00:11:30.256 --> 00:11:31.150
They're hoping to learn

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.100
about how our brains change

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.433
by studying expert wayfinders.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:39.250
We're taking a much more detailed look

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:40.740
at the cabbie hippocampus

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.630
than has ever been done before.

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:47.630
It is helping us to learn how
the cabbie hippocampus grows.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.820
The task that we have taxi drivers perform

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:54.289
is very similar to the job
that they do on a daily basis.

00:11:54.289 --> 00:11:56.657
The game will begin
again in a few seconds,

00:11:56.657 --> 00:11:58.981
with another 15 routes.

00:11:58.981 --> 00:12:00.550
Ok.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.930
We essentially have them
do a route planning task

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.330
where we tell them a point A to a point B,

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.760
ask them to plan this route in their head,

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.767
and then simply ask them
a yes or no question:

00:12:09.767 --> 00:12:12.230
"Would you take street X?"

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.390
And then they respond,

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:15.110
"yes" or "no", they would take that street

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:17.270
along the route that they
planned in their head.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:19.180
The design of this task was developed

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:23.770
to actually test a mathematical theory

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:26.900
of how they might be able to plan

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:30.200
over this huge structure in state space

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.950
that is the 30,000 streets of London.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.990
They can't possibly just be planning

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:38.390
across all of the streets,

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.260
all of the possible connections.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.090
First of all, that
would take way too long,

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:44.900
and these taxi drivers plan their route

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.200
in about three seconds.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:48.610
At least, they say they do.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:52.000
When you come to the window,
tell them where you wanna go,

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.940
they think, "Ok, I know where to go."

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.350
And then they take you there

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.750
and simply there has to be
a way for them to reduce

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:01.320
this massive cognitive demand of this.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:02.250
It's actually interesting.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.880
As we've talked to a
lot of taxi drivers now,

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.020
they've informed us that the
most difficult routes for them

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:09.760
are actually the shortest routes,

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:13.320
the ones that are within a local area,

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:16.220
because those routes are actually,

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:19.520
they have the least margin of error there.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.780
They pretty much do actually
have to plan the entire route

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:24.820
from street A to street B,

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.920
whereas when they have a longer journey,

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.180
they're able to more easily
compartmentalize that route

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:35.120
into a series of global segments.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.070
London taxi drivers are
this very famous example

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.730
of adult plasticity in the brain,

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:44.620
and specifically, adult
plasticity in the hippocampus,

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.830
which is the region that is
one of the first to be impacted

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.250
by the onset of Alzheimer's disease

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:51.698
and we're hoping that that will give us

00:13:51.698 --> 00:13:55.690
a better understanding
of how this area grows

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:58.800
before it's started to decline,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:00.660
and that that will give us, for one,

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.380
just a better understanding
of the hippocampus in general,

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:05.510
but then also, down the line,

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.860
whether or not doing something like this

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.000
and learning the Knowledge,

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.420
becoming a taxi driver,

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:11.770
growing your hippocampus,

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.760
having this adult plasticity,

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.110
whether that gives you,

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:17.750
does it give you a buffer?

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:21.470
Does it help you prevent
Alzheimer's disease?

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:25.403
Those are questions that we
don't know the answers to.

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:29.370
While navigational skills
are clearly useful,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.320
do the majority of us really need them

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.540
in a world where simple journey planning

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.140
can easily be done by apps on our phones?

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:37.430
Every piece of technology comes

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.670
with its own pros and cons, right?

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:42.790
and having Sat Nav, using Google Maps

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.669
or Apple Maps, or whatever,

00:14:44.669 --> 00:14:48.710
it is great in the sense
that it does help people

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:50.910
who might not be a great navigator,

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.650
or you're visiting a new place.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.510
It can be very helpful in that sense.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:57.500
You're essentially reducing
the cognitive load for yourself

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.680
by offloading it to this app,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.780
but in doing so,

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:04.810
you're not training
your brain, essentially,

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:08.760
so you're not gonna learn that route.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.920
We most definitely convince ourselves

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.430
that we can't figure it out.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:13.730
We have a cognitive block.

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:16.120
We often don't think we can do something.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:19.178
Our ability to navigate is kind of

00:15:19.178 --> 00:15:20.510
fundamental to survival,

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.340
so while we think and many people argue,

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:28.450
that GPS is kind of getting
rid of that ability,

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:30.340
everything that we've studied says

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:33.740
it's like it's born with every child

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:37.180
and it's a matter of merely
exercising that muscle.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:38.560
If you're hoping to get there.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:40.090
you'll definitely get there.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:41.410
If you're hoping to train your brain

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:43.600
and become a better navigator,

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.481
it's probably not the best way to go.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.230
Smart design can encourage us to wayfind

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.100
and strengthen our natural
navigational skills.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.060
While you probably
shouldn't delete those apps

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.280
that use GPS just yet,

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.425
it might be worth traveling a part

00:16:01.425 --> 00:16:02.790
of your next journey without it.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.391
Who knows, you might grow
a hippocampus like James.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.820
My idea of fun on a weekend away somewhere

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:15.910
is to get myself lost
and then find my way out,

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:19.830
so I will deliberately
find the back streets

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.070
and then I'll work out how to
get back to where I wanna be

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:23.370
and I'm pretty good at it.

